P0074 DATA padding consumes flow-control credit without returning it


P0075 [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:580).


P0076 HTTP/2 flow control counts the entire DATA payload, including the padding-length octet and padding. This code counts only the application payload after padding has been stripped.


P0077 Executed evidence: with a 256-byte stream window, a legal DATA frame containing one padding-length octet and 255 padding octets produced an empty application buffer. h2 reported available capacity of 256 and used capacity of zero, and sent no WINDOW_UPDATE within the test’s 200 ms timeout. The peer has exhausted its window and cannot continue the body.


P0078 account for the original DATA payload length and automatically release discarded padding on both stream and connection windows. The existing `data_padding` test verifies body contents without checking credit accounting.


P0079 Regression: [padding.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/padding.rs).


P0080 Oversized trailers silently lose fields


P0081 [src/proto/streams/recv.rs:361](/work/crate/src/proto/streams/recv.rs:361).


P0082 Header decoding marks oversized blocks and omits fields beyond the configured limit. Initial-header handling checks that marker; trailer handling ignores it and delivers the remaining fields successfully.


P0083 Executed evidence: with `max_header_list_size(64)`, trailers containing `checksum` with a 100-byte value returned `Ok(Some({}))`. A caller receives successful completion with missing metadata.


P0084 reject oversized trailers before delivering them, consistently with oversized initial headers.


P0085 Regression: [trailers.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/trailers.rs).


P0086 A response without `:status` becomes HTTP 200


P0087 [src/client.rs:1676](/work/crate/src/client.rs:1676).


P0088 A response must contain the mandatory `:status` pseudo-header. When it is absent, this conversion leaves `http::Response::builder()` at its default status.


P0089 Executed evidence: a response header block containing only `content-length: 0` and END_STREAM returned a successful HTTP 200 response. Callers can therefore mistake a malformed response for success.


P0090 require `pseudo.status` and return a stream protocol error when absent.


P0091 Regression: [status.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/status.rs).


P0092 Large capacity reservations wrap to zero


P0093 [src/share.rs:287](/work/crate/src/share.rs:287).


P0094 `reserve_capacity` accepts `usize` but casts directly to the `u32` window-size type. Its documentation specifies no corresponding argument limit.


P0095 Executed evidence: reservations of 1,024 and 4,294,967,295 bytes received capacity; 4,294,967,296 received zero, and 4,294,967,297 received one byte. Reserving a large remaining file size requires no large allocation, so an ordinary streaming caller can encounter this boundary and wait indefinitely for capacity.


P0096 retain the demand as `usize`, or clamp it before narrowing.


P0097 Regression: [capacity.rs](/work/tmp/probes/tests/capacity.rs).


P0098 Header lists exactly at the advertised limit are rejected


P0099 Locations: [src/frame/headers.rs:869](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:869), [src/frame/headers.rs:911](/work/crate/src/frame/headers.rs:911).


P0100 Both comparisons use `< max_header_list_size`, rejecting equality despite the documented maximum.


P0101 Executed evidence: `:status: 200` occupies 42 decoded header-list bytes. It succeeded with limit 43 and failed with `PROTOCOL_ERROR` at limit 42. Adding `x: a` produced the same result at 77 versus its exact size of 76.


P0102 Consequently, a peer respecting the advertised maximum can have a valid response rejected.


P0103 accept equality in both comparisons and add boundary regressions.


